##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    1125 1451  219
                                                                  1125 1451  220
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       1125 1451  221
                                                                  1125 1451  222
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          1125 1451  223
                                                                  1125 1451  224
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   1125 1451  225
                                                                  1125 1451  226
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 1125 1451  227
                                                                  1125 1451  228
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     1125 1451  229
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   1125 1451  230
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             1125 1451  231
                                                                  1125 1451  232
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                1125 1451  233
                                                                  1125 1451  234
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       1125 1451  235
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  1125 1451  236
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   1125 1451  237
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  1125 1451  238
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  1125 1451  239
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 1125 1451  240
to the highest incident energy.                                   1125 1451  241
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:1125 1451  242
                                                                  1125 1451  243
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  1125 1451  244
                                                                  1125 1451  245
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    1125 1451  246
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     1125 1451  247
                                                                  1125 1451  248
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     1125 1451  249
                                                                  1125 1451  250
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    1125 1451  251
total cross section.                                              1125 1451  252
                                                                  1125 1451  253
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            1125 1451  254
                                                                  1125 1451  255
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    1125 1451  256
                                                                  1125 1451  257
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        1125 1451  258
                                                                  1125 1451  259
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  1125 1451  260
                                                                  1125 1451  261
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           1125 1451  262
                                                                  1125 1451  263
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   1125 1451  264
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        1125 1451  265
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     1125 1451  266
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         1125 1451  267
sections.                                                         1125 1451  268
                                                                  1125 1451  269
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 1125 1451  270
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 1125 1451  271
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 1125 1451  272
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 1125 1451  273
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      1125 1451  274
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       1125 1451  275
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              1125 1451  276
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  1125 1451  277
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  1125 1451  278
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  1125 1451  279
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  1125 1451  280
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  1125 1451  281
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1125 1451  282
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        1125 1451  283
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1125 1451  284
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        1125 1451  285
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1125 1451  286
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        1125 1451  287
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1125 1451  288
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        1125 1451  289
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1125 1451  290
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        1125 1451  291
                                                                  1125 1451  292
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           1125 1451  293
                                                                  1125 1451  294
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    1125 1451  295
discrete levels.                                                  1125 1451  296
                                                                  1125 1451  297
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         1125 1451  298
                                                                  1125 1451  299
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  1125 1451  300
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   1125 1451  301
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      1125 1451  302
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  1125 1451  303
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      1125 1451  304
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         1125 1451  305
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   1125 1451  306
on an angular grid.                                               1125 1451  307
                                                                  1125 1451  308
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       1125 1451  309
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1125 1451  310
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1125 1451  311
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1125 1451  312
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1125 1451  313
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1125 1451  314
                                                                  1125 1451  315
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     1125 1451  316
                                                                  1125 1451  317
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     1125 1451  318
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   1125 1451  319
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  1125 1451  320
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             1125 1451  321
All data are generated with TALYS.                                1125 1451  322
                                                                  1125 1451  323
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   1125 1451  324
                                                                  1125 1451  325
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      1125 1451  326
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   1125 1451  327
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   1125 1451  328
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are1125 1451  329
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   1125 1451  330
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  1125 1451  331
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              1125 1451  332
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 1125 1451  333
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  1125 1451  334
For each residual product:                                        1125 1451  335
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               1125 1451  336
- the recoil                                                      1125 1451  337
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         1125 1451  338
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     1125 1451  339
sections.                                                         1125 1451  340
                                                                  1125 1451  341
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     1125 1451  342
                                                                  1125 1451  343
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  1125 1451  344
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 1125 1451  345
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          1125 1451  346
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       1125 1451  347
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 1125 1451  348
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     1125 1451  349
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  1125 1451  350
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      1125 1451  351
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         1125 1451  352
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             1125 1451  353
                                                                  1125 1451  354
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   1125 1451  355
followed:                                                         1125 1451  356
                                                                  1125 1451  357
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1125 1451  358
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1125 1451  359
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1125 1451  360
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     1125 1451  361
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1125 1451  362
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1125 1451  363
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1125 1451  364
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1125 1451  365
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1125 1451  366
                                                                  1125 1451  367
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 1125 1451  368
                                                                  1125 1451  369
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    1125 1451  370
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          1125 1451  371
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     1125 1451  372
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          1125 1451  373
                                                                  1125 1451  374
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  1125 1451  375
                                                                  1125 1451  376
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     1125 1451  377
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1125 1451  378
as isomer.                                                        1125 1451  379
                                                                  1125 1451  380
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 1125 1451  381
                                                                  1125 1451  382
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        1125 1451  383
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1125 1451  384
as isomer.                                                        1125 1451  385
                                                                  1125 1451  386
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          1125 1451  387
                                                                  1125 1451  388
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:1125 1451  389
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   1125 1451  390
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   1125 1451  391
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   1125 1451  392
  given                                                           1125 1451  393
                                                                  1125 1451  394
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          1125 1451  395
                                                                  1125 1451  396
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  1125 1451  397
                                                                  1125 1451  398
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 1125 1451  399
                                                                  1125 1451  400
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         1125 1451  401
                                                                  1125 1451  402
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   1125 1451  403
                                                                  1125 1451  404
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        1125 1451  405
and more details are given above in this description. In most     1125 1451  406
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        1125 1451  407
                                                                  1125 1451  408
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 1125 1451  409
                                                                  1125 1451  410
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  1125 1451  411
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     1125 1451  412
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    1125 1451  413
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   1125 1451  414
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  1125 1451  415
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                1125 1451  416
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      1125 1451  417
   same reaction channel between different energies)              1125 1451  418
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      1125 1451  419
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               1125 1451  420
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      1125 1451  421
   different reaction channels for different energies)            1125 1451  422
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        1125 1451  423
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              1125 1451  424
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  1125 1451  425
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       1125 1451  426
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     1125 1451  427
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  1125 1451  428
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     1125 1451  429
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     1125 1451  430
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     1125 1451  431
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   1125 1451  432
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   1125 1451  433
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  1125 1451  434
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   1125 1451  435
                                                                  1125 1451  436
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  1125 1451  437
                                                                  1125 1451  438
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       1125 1451  439
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  1125 1451  440
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       1125 1451  441
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   1125 1451  442
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         1125 1451  443
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  1125 1451  444
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      1125 1451  445
representation is used.                                           1125 1451  446
                                                                  1125 1451  447
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      1125 1451  448
                                                                  1125 1451  449
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  1125 1451  450
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             1125 1451  451
                                                                  1125 1451  452
